· Own the end-to-end GTM strategy for key product and feature launches, from brief to post-launch measurement;

· Introduce new frameworks and playbooks that elevate how EBANX brings products to market;

· Sit at the table with Product, Commercial, and Marketing leadership — not just execute on their decisions, but shape them;

· Build repeatable, scalable GTM infrastructure in a high-growth global payments company;

· Have significant creative latitude in how stories are told, formats are designed, and launches are structured.

For someone who thrives on driving visible impact — and who gets restless without room to experiment — this role offers genuine ownership and career-defining exposure.

· Lead go-to-market planning and execution for product launches across markets, verticals, and payment methods — from positioning strategy through launch and measurement;

· Propose and implement new GTM formats, launch playbooks, and cross-functional rituals that improve speed, alignment, and impact;

· Align internal stakeholders — Product, Commercial, and Marketing — ensuring launches land consistently and coherently across the organization;

· Translate complex payment solutions into compelling, value-driven narratives and enablement content that equips commercial teams to sell effectively;

· Monitor market and competitor landscape to continuously sharpen EBANX's product positioning and differentiation;

· Act as a strategic connector, bringing market and customer insights directly into the roadmap and commercial conversations.

What you need to shine in this role

· Experience leading go-to-market strategies and product launches — ideally in B2B, fintech, or global tech environments;

· Proven track record of driving revenue-contributing launches with minimal supervision, from strategy through execution;

· Strong stakeholder management skills: able to align diverse and global teams, drive consensus, and keep launches on track without direct authority;

· Creative thinking paired with structured execution — you bring new ideas and follow through on them;

· Ability to craft clear, compelling narratives from complex product and market information;

· Data-driven mindset: you measure what you launch and use results to improve the next cycle;

· Comfortable working independently in a fast-paced, global environment with multiple stakeholders and shifting priorities;

· Fluency in English (written and spoken).

· Experience in the payments industry or digital commerce;

· Background in building or redesigning GTM processes from scratch;

· Familiarity with product management methodologies or close collaboration with product teams;

· Experience creating merchant-facing or sales enablement materials;

· Track record of introducing new working methods that were adopted across teams.

Licenciatura / Graduación en Ingeniería, Economía u otras carreras de perfil analítico;
Fuertes habilidades analíticas para interpretar datos y tomar decisiones basadas en números;
Experiencia consolidada en Growth Marketing, preferiblemente con una sólida trayectoria táctica en la operación de campañas de adquisición pagada (Facebook Ads, Google Ads, etc.);
Conocimiento sólido de métricas de performance (CPC, CPA, ROI, ROAS, CTR, etc.);
Experiencia con herramientas de analítica, como Google Analytics, Amplitude o similares;
Conocimiento en lenguajes de programación para el desarrollo de análisis, como SQL o Python;
Inglés avanzado.

Own and drive customer marketing initiatives end-to-end, from identifying high-leverage cohorts to launching campaigns built on your messaging and creative directions;
Develop messaging and positioning for existing customers, with a particular focus on driving awareness and adoption of the Manager Agent among our existing base;
Build a content engine rooted in customer activity: surface stories, proof points, and product-in-action moments that feed back into the broader marketing system;
Carry new product and feature launches to the existing base, turning each release into adoption and deeper utilization of what customers already have;
Be the voice of the customer internally. Feed what you learn back to product, Customer Success, and marketing;
Collaborate with Customer Success and Sales to identify at-risk accounts, expansion opportunities, and customers ready for the next step — building the messaging, content, and plays that protect retention and drive upsell.
Design and execute engagement programs across owned channels (email, in-product, events, webinars) to turn happy customers into references and advocates.
Use AI to scale your output without compromising quality — automating what can be automated, moving faster, and elevating everything you produce above the generic AI baseline;
Track and report on commercial impact: deals influenced, expansion pipeline touched, and CSM-attributed activity driven by customer marketing.
No two weeks look alike, but most start with a signal. A conversation with a CSM surfaces a pattern across a handful of accounts, and that becomes the seed of a campaign. By midweek, you're briefing creative on it and lining up a webinar you'll run with an internal expert to back it. You'll have one program live, another taking shape, a clear read on what the last one did, and a rough idea of how you'll reframe the next newsletter.
You move between the thinking and the doing, and you don't wait for either to be handed to you.
Your team
You'll be joining a small, high-ownership marketing team. This means that everybody owns their function and leads their expertise with autonomy and accountability. You will have influence.
You'll work cross-functionally with Customer Success, Product, and Sales, those relationships are essential to doing this job well. The team moves fast, communicates often, and defaults to action over process. Nobody is waiting to be told what to do next.
Workleap is entering a pivotal phase, bringing an agentic product offering to market while deepening value for our existing customer base. Customer marketing at Workleap is yours to shape from the ground up, which means the person in this role has the rare opportunity to define what good looks like.
